{
  "dom_id": "step-e6103",
  "name": "Example: Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the parabola $y=x^2$ and the line $y=4?$\n\n- It is a type I region\n\n- It is a type II region\n\n- It's neither a type I nor a type II region",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that\n\n$$\ny=x^2\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx = \\pm\\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038811/assets/59d32c44bae848d52adc188e15a8bab470b3eccd887cad45c231b66c486284fc.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- Any vertical line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: x^2 \\leq y \\leq 4 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type I region.\n\n- Any horizontal line through the region intersects it on a single interval. Furthermore, we can represent our region as\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 4, \\: -\\sqrt{y} \\leq x \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, this is a type II region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
